<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A shape is transformed by <m>A = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 3 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}</m>, and its image is then transformed by <m>B = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0 &amp; 1 \\ -1 &amp; 1 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</p><p>(a) Find the single matrix that maps the final image back onto the original shape.</p><div><fillin characters="3" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) A vertex of the final image is <m>({-6},\,{-4})</m>. Find the original vertex.</p><div>( <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> , <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/> )   </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Going forwards, the single matrix is <m>BA = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 3 &amp; -1 \\ 2 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}</m>. To undo two transformations, undo the last one first: <m>B^{-1}</m> followed by <m>A^{-1}</m>, whose matrix is <m>A^{-1}B^{-1} = (BA)^{-1}</m>.</p><p><m>(BA)^{-1} = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; -1 \\ 2 &amp; -3 \end{array}\right]}</m>, which is also <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 3 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; -1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; -1 \\ 2 &amp; -3 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{c} -6 \\ -4 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{c} -2 \\ 0 \end{array}\right]}</m>, so the original vertex is <m>({-2},\,{0})</m>. Check: <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 3 &amp; -1 \\ 2 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{c} -2 \\ 0 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{c} -6 \\ -4 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; -1 \\ 2 &amp; -3 \end{array}\right]</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-2</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>0</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>